course: arts
lesson: 18
title: Modern art: "my little brother could do that"
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Modern art is a loose name for art from about the 1860s to the 1970s, and people often use it for newer art too; much of it cares more about an idea or a feeling than about being pretty.
explain: Picture Alfie at the Tate Modern scoffing at a canvas of one colour: copying it is easy now, but the artist was among the first to try the idea, and people still argue whether it is great art; both opinions are allowed.
explain: Some modern art is a puzzle about what art even is: a shop-bought urinal signed with a fake name, a banana taped to a wall, a pile of sweets visitors may take, or a dark room of mirrors and lights that seems to go on for ever.
explain: A fair test is to give it a real look first: stand in front of it for a minute, ask what idea the artist was testing and what it makes you think, then decide; art can aim to be beautiful, but also to shock, ask questions, make people laugh or help them remember.
explain: People react in their own slang: "you're having a laugh" means you must be joking, "dodgy" means suspicious, "it's giving space" means it feels like outer space, "pet" in the North East means dear, and "bruv" in London means mate.
fact: Abstract art does not show recognisable things; some artists painted one colour or simple shapes on purpose to make people think about colour, size and feeling.
fact: Whether such paintings are great art is argued: some say they are powerful in real life, others say they are a joke.
fact: In 1917 Marcel Duchamp sent a shop-bought urinal, signed "R. Mutt", to an art show, asking who decides what art is; an everyday object shown as art is called a readymade.
fact: Jackson Pollock laid huge canvases on the floor and dripped and flung paint with control; people still argue about the skill, and painters who try to copy it usually find it hard.
fact: In 2019 Maurizio Cattelan showed a real banana taped to a wall, called Comedian; in 2024 one sold at auction for about 6.2 million dollars, and the buyer gets a certificate and replaces the banana when it rots.
fact: People argue whether the banana is a clever joke about money and art or proof that the art market is silly.
fact: Louise Bourgeois made giant spider sculptures called Maman, French for mummy; she said the spider stood for her mother, who repaired tapestries, patient and protective.
fact: The Japanese artist Yayoi Kusama makes Infinity Mirror Rooms where mirrors and lights make the space look endless; art that fills a room is called an installation.
fact: In the 1960s Andy Warhol and other Pop artists made art from adverts, famous faces and supermarket products such as soup cans, printing images again and again.
fact: Felix Gonzalez-Torres made a pile of sweets weighing the same as his partner, who was ill; visitors may take sweets, the pile shrinks, and the gallery refills it.
fact: Minimalism is art that uses very little, like simple shapes; whether a nearly empty work is deep or a con is argued.
fact: Art can aim to be beautiful, but also to shock, ask questions, make people laugh or remember something.
exercise: Alfie says his little brother could paint the one-colour canvas. What is fair to say? | The artist tried the idea first, and people still argue about it | It is definitely the greatest painting ever made, and everyone knows it | It is definitely not art, and nobody disagrees | His little brother painted it in secret one day after school and sold it
exercise: What is abstract art? | Art that does not show recognisable things | Art made only of photographs of real places | Art painted only by children | Art that must always show real people and their faces
exercise: Chloe's dad laughs at the urinal artwork. What was Duchamp asking? | Who decides what art is | How to fix a broken bathroom tap | Whether toilets should be painted gold | How much a toilet costs in a shop
exercise: What name did Duchamp sign on the urinal? | R. Mutt | P. Picasso | A. Warhol | B. Banksy
exercise: Noah says Pollock just dropped paint. What is true? | He dripped and flung paint with control, and copying it is hard | He dropped paint by accident one day and then decided to call it art | He painted with a ruler and tiny brushes | He let his dog walk across the canvas with paint on its paws
exercise: Jess's uncle calls the taped banana "dodgy". What is true? | People argue whether it is a clever joke or shows the art market is silly | Everyone agrees it is the best artwork ever made, and nobody laughs at it | The banana was made of solid gold | The banana has been on the wall for 100 years
exercise: What does the buyer of the banana artwork get? | A certificate, and they replace the banana when it rots | The same banana, kept fresh for ever in a special glass box | A painting of a banana | Nothing at all, not even a paper
exercise: Lola thinks the giant spider is scary. What did the artist say it meant? | Her own mother, patient and protective | A monster from a nightmare she had as a little girl | A warning about real spiders | Nothing at all, it is random
exercise: What does "Maman" mean in French? | Mummy | Spider | Monster | House
exercise: Ivan steps into a room of mirrors and lights. What is it? | An installation, like Kusama's Infinity Mirror Rooms | A broken lift in the gallery that nobody has fixed | A storeroom with spare lights | A shop selling mirrors and lamps to the visitors
exercise: Ruby asks why Warhol painted soup cans. What was the point? | To show everyday, mass-produced life as art | He was paid by the soup company to advertise it | He only owned soup cans | He thought soup was the most serious subject in the world
exercise: Theo panics after taking a sweet from the pile. What is true? | Visitors are allowed, and taking part is part of the art | He has stolen a priceless artwork and his parents must pay for it | The sweets are fake and made of plastic | Touching any art is always forbidden, in every gallery
exercise: What did the weight of the sweet pile match? | The artist's partner, who was ill | The artist's car | A baby elephant at the zoo | One whole year of sweets
exercise: Hamza laughs at the blank white canvas. What is a fair test? | Ask what it makes you think about | Laugh, because it is definitely a con | Paint over it with colours to improve it | Ignore it without looking at all
exercise: Lucas says modern art isn't real because it isn't pretty. What is true? | Art can aim to shock, question, amuse or remember, not only please | Art must always be pretty to count, or galleries will not hang it | Only old art is real art | Pretty art is never allowed in galleries of modern art, by their rules
test: Your mate goes, "art has to be made by hand or it don't count, innit." Who argued otherwise? | Duchamp, with a shop-bought object shown as art | Michelangelo, with the Sistine ceiling | Van Gogh, with his sunflowers painted for a friend | Leonardo, with his notebooks of machines and drawings
test: What would happen if you tried to copy a Pollock with a bucket of paint? | You would most likely find it harder than it looks | It would come out exactly the same, first time | Pollock would sue you from the past | The paint would refuse to drip onto the canvas at all
test: Ruby's gran says, "That was the whole point, pet." What did Warhol want to show? | That everyday shop things can be art | That soup is healthy for everyone | That cans are dangerous | That painting is boring and old
test: Ivan whispers, "Yo, this is giving space." What does he mean? | It feels like being in outer space | It needs a lot more space in it | He wants to leave | It is very crowded with people
test: A cartoon shows a guard tackling a kid who took a sweet from the Gonzalez-Torres pile. What is wrong? | Visitors are allowed to take sweets from that work | Nothing, sweets in galleries are never allowed | The guard should have taken a sweet too, and shared it | The sweets are made of glass and cannot be eaten
test: Chloe's dad says, "A toilet? You're having a laugh." What is the fairest answer? | Many still disagree, but it asked who decides what art is | He is wrong, because absolutely everyone agrees that it is great | He is right, nobody talks about it now | It was a joke that nobody noticed at the time or since
test: Lucas's cousin says, "Bruv, art's not only about pretty." What does "bruv" mean here? | Mate, a friendly word | Brother, his real sibling only | A teacher | A stranger
test: Lola is scared of the giant spider. What would help her see it differently? | Learning that it stood for the artist's caring mother | Being told to close her eyes and walk past | Knowing that it is made of real spiders | Nothing, it is only about fear and nightmares
test: Alfie says "My little brother could do that." What is the best first step before deciding? | Stand and look at it for a minute | Walk past it quickly without looking | Shout that it is rubbish | Touch it to see how thick the paint is
test: Jess's uncle asks who'd pay millions for a banana. Which is a fair view? | Some see a joke about money and art, others think the market is silly | Everyone agrees it was worth every penny, and nobody has ever laughed at it | Nobody has ever paid anything at all for it | Bananas are always that expensive in galleries
test: What would happen if you waited for the original banana to last for ever? | It would rot, so the owner replaces it | It would stay fresh and yellow for ever | It would turn into gold | It would grow into a banana tree
exam: Identify the principal question raised by the exhibition in 1917 of a shop-bought urinal by Marcel Duchamp. | The question of who determines what constitutes art | The question of the correct maintenance of sanitary fittings | The question of the cost of manufactured goods | The question of the colour appropriate to a bathroom
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the artwork entitled Comedian by Maurizio Cattelan. | It consists of a banana affixed to a wall, the purchaser replacing the banana as it decays | It consists of a banana preserved without decay in perpetuity by a secret chemical process | It consists of a painting of a banana upon canvas | It has never been sold by any means
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the critical reception of monochrome and minimalist works? | Their merit is disputed, some regarding them as profound and others as a jest | They are universally acknowledged as the finest works of art ever produced by any hand | They are universally rejected as not constituting art | They have attracted no opinion of any kind
exam: Ascertain the meaning attributed by Louise Bourgeois to her spider sculptures entitled Maman. | Her mother, patient and protective in the manner of a spider spinning | A monster encountered in a nightmare of her childhood, which she wished to forget | A warning concerning venomous spiders | No meaning whatsoever
